{"name":"Mineral","hex":"#d7d1c5","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"Mineral","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Proper noun","definitions":["A number of places in the United States, including:\nA census-designated place in Tehama County, California.\nA town in Louisa County, Virginia.","A census-designated place in Tehama County, California."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/Mineral"}],"entries":[{"title":"Mineral","blocks":[{"text":"In geology and mineralogy, a mineral or mineral species is, broadly speaking, a solid substance with a fairly well-defined chemical composition and a specific crystal structure that occurs naturally in pure form."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/50/S%C3%A9randite%2C_natrolite%2C_analcime%2C_aegirine_300-4-2112.JPG/330px-S%C3%A9randite%2C_natrolite%2C_analcime%2C_aegirine_300-4-2112.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mineral","wikidata":"Q7946"},{"title":"Mineral (nutrient)","blocks":[{"text":"In the context of nutrition, a mineral is a chemical element. Some \"minerals\" are essential for life, but most are not. Minerals are one of the four groups of essential nutrients; the others are vitamins, essential fatty acids, and essential amino acids. The five major minerals in the human body are calcium, phosphorus, potassium, sodium, and magnesium. The remaining minerals are called \"trace elements\". The generally accepted trace elements are iron, chlorine, cobalt, copper, zinc, manganese, molybdenum, iodine, selenium, and bromine; there is some evidence that there may be more."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d2/Human_carbonic_anhydrase_II_PDB%3D6LUX.png/330px-Human_carbonic_anhydrase_II_PDB%3D6LUX.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mineral_(nutrient)","wikidata":"Q189598"}],"images":[{"srcset":"http